  "title": "ViewTouch (1986)",
  "year": "1986",
  "subtitle": "The first commercial graphical touchscreen point-of-sale system — finger-on-glass order entry replacing the physical register, built on an Atari ST with a MicroTouch capacitive overlay.",
  "why_scout_cares": "ViewTouch is the museum's only retail/hospitality point-of-sale artifact and its first artifact where a consumer-facing worker runs a full business on a touchscreen. The interaction model is pure direct manipulation: waitstaff tap finger-tip zones on a capacitive overlay to place orders, reconfiguring an Atari ST into a register. It is the ancestor of every modern restaurant/pardon touchscreen POS, and it was built in 1986 by a restaurateur writing his own software — the digital register as personal craft. Distinct from every touchscreen in the museum (HP-150 desktop, Buick GCC dashboard, Tektronix) because the touching is done by production staff to drive a live business, and the screen is the entire machine.",
