  "research": {
    "title": "ViewTouch (1986)",
    "year": "1986",
    "by": "Gene Mosher (Syracuse, NY)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Touchscreen", "Retail", "Point of Sale", "Direct Manipulation"],
    "overview": "ViewTouch is the first commercially deployed graphical touchscreen point-of-sale system. In 1986 restaurateur Gene Mosher bought an Atari ST computer and a MicroTouch resistive-capacitive touchscreen overlay from his Old Canal Cafe in Syracuse, New York, and wrote POS software that let waitstaff take orders by directly touching on-screen buttons — menu categories, items, modifiers, and table maps — replacing the paper ticket and the keypad electronic cash register. The hardware was a retail machine rather than a home computer: an Atari 520ST/1040ST (Motorola 68000) running under a 12-inch Atari SC1224 color CRT fitted with the MicroTouch overlay, mounted in a custom wooden enclosure with a Star Micronics DP8340 printer on top. Mosher had written his first POS software for the Apple II in 1978 (he owned Apple II serial #753 from the first manufacturing run).\n\nThe photo on Wikimedia Commons, taken by Barbara Mosher at the Atari booth at Comdex in Las Vegas on 17 November 1986, shows Mosher with a running ViewTouch system and is the primary image for the exhibit. ViewTouch went on to be used in dozens of restaurants and bars, and Mosher continued developing it for decades; today it survives as an open-source Linux/Raspberry Pi hospitality platform under the GNU GPL. The embodied interaction — staff driving a live business by fingertip on a color display, with no keyboard — is the direct ancestor of every modern touchscreen POS terminal.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Origins",
        "text": "Mosher bought an Apple II in 1977 (serial 753) and began writing point-of-sale software for his restaurants in July 1978, running order-taking on an Apple II at the entrance of his Old Canal Cafe and printing cooking instructions for the kitchen. In 1986 he bought an Atari ST and a MicroTouch touchscreen overlay and built ViewTouch, the first commercial restaurant/bar POS using a graphical touchscreen interface, trademarking the name ViewTouch. The date 1986 is corroborated by the dated Comdex photograph (17 November 1986); some secondary sources say 1985, but Mosher's own timeline and the dated photo pin the commercial system to 1986."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The interaction model",
        "text": "ViewTouch is a pure direct-manipulation interface: waitstaff press finger-tip zones directly on a capacitive touch overlay mounted over an Atari ST's color display. There is no keyboard and no stylus — order entry is entirely by touching on-screen areas for menu categories, individual items, modifiers, and table maps. The screen IS the register and the checkout is a fistful of finger taps. The widget engine Mosher wrote for it is an unusually early direct-manipulation application framework. This pre-figures tablet-based restaurant ordering by two decades."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Significance",
        "text": "ViewTouch is a missing link in the story of touchscreen HCI: where most early touch machines were kiosks or lab instruments (HP-150 desktop computer, Buick Riviera dashboard), ViewTouch put a touchscreen in the hands of production service staff to drive a live business in real time — order entry, modifiers, table mapping, kitchen printing. It is the direct ancestor of the modern restaurant POS and of the tablet ordering that now dominates hospitality. It also embodies personal-scale computing: one businessman built the software, the hardware stack, and the deployment from a single well-furnished restaurant."
      }
    ],
